  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>Safeguarding is the first thing an inspection interrogates, and rightly so: it goes to whether the people in a home are safe from abuse and neglect. A safeguarding audit is how a registered manager checks, before the inspector does, that concerns are being raised and followed up, that the training and authorisations are in place, and that the paper trail behind all of it would stand up. The real question is not what to check, the duties are well known, but how to run the audit so the evidence is honest, current and assembled before anyone asks.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>A safeguarding audit checks that concerns are recognised, referred, logged and followed up, and that the surrounding controls, training, policy and authorisations, are in place.</li><li>The legal framework differs by nation: the Care Act 2014 in England, the Adult Support and Protection (Scotland) Act 2007 in Scotland, with Wales and Northern Ireland having their own.</li><li>Deprivation of liberty is authorised through DoLS in England and separate arrangements in Scotland; the audit checks these are in place and in date.</li><li>Inspectors weight safeguarding heavily, under CQC's safe key question and the equivalent in the other nations' frameworks.</li><li>The audit does not keep people safe; the staff acting on it do, and the registered manager owns the actions.</li><li>Quickler records the audit and the evidence. It is not a care record and does not hold the detail of a safeguarding case about a named resident.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The duty","heading":"The safeguarding framework across the UK","html":"<p>Safeguarding adults sits on different statutes across the UK. In England, the Care Act 2014 places duties on local authorities and expects providers to work within local multi-agency safeguarding arrangements. In Scotland, the Adult Support and Protection (Scotland) Act 2007 sets the duties to inquire and to protect adults at risk of harm. Wales and Northern Ireland have their own frameworks. Whatever the nation, a care home must recognise concerns, raise them without delay to the right body, cooperate with any inquiry, and keep its own residents safe in the meantime.</p><p>A safeguarding audit tests that this works in practice, not just in policy. It checks that staff know what a concern looks like and how to raise it, that referrals were made promptly and logged, that actions were owned and closed, and that the home learns from what happened. Always work from your local safeguarding arrangements and the current statutory guidance, and treat this page as general information, not legal advice.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Liberty and training","heading":"Authorisations, training and competence","html":"<p>Two controls deserve their own attention. Deprivation of liberty: where a resident is under continuous supervision and not free to leave, the home needs the right authorisation in place and in date, through the Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards in England or the separate arrangements in Scotland. The audit checks each is current and that conditions are being met. Training: safeguarding training has to be current across the whole team, including bank and agency staff, and, crucially, staff have to be able to act on it. An audit that only checks a training date, and never checks whether a care worker could recognise and report a concern, checks the wrong thing.</p><p>Quickler records the state of each control, the referral log reviewed, the authorisation confirmed in date, the training currency checked, with a photo of the record where it helps. It does not hold the case detail about a named resident; that stays in your care and safeguarding records. Quickler captures the audit finding and the evidence that the control is in place, and flags the overdue ones so they surface rather than slip.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Scope, honestly","heading":"Auditing the system, not the case","html":"<p>The honest scope matters more here than anywhere. A safeguarding case involves highly sensitive personal information about a resident, and that belongs in your secure care and safeguarding records, held to the standards those demand. Quickler does not hold it and should not.</p><p>What quickler does is audit the <em>system</em>: is there a policy, is training current, are referrals being logged and closed, are authorisations in date, is there evidence of learning from incidents? The auditor records the finding, that the safeguarding log was reviewed and referrals were made and followed up appropriately, without copying case detail into quickler. The care record and the safeguarding file stay the single source of truth. Quickler is the audit and evidence layer that shows the system around them is working.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>What does a care home safeguarding audit cover?</h3><p>It checks that the home recognises, raises, logs and follows up safeguarding concerns, and that the controls around this are in place: a current policy, safeguarding training across the team, deprivation of liberty authorisations in date, and evidence of learning from incidents. It tests the system and the paper trail, not the detail of any individual case.</p><h3>Which law applies to safeguarding in a care home?</h3><p>It depends on the nation. In England the Care Act 2014, in Scotland the Adult Support and Protection (Scotland) Act 2007, with Wales and Northern Ireland having their own frameworks, all sitting alongside local multi-agency safeguarding arrangements. Work from your local arrangements and the current statutory guidance, and treat this as general information, not legal advice.</p><h3>Does Quickler store our safeguarding case records?</h3><p>No. A safeguarding case involves sensitive personal information about a resident, and that belongs in your secure care and safeguarding records. Quickler audits the system around it, that referrals are logged and followed up, training is current, authorisations are in date, and records the finding and evidence without copying case detail into quickler.</p><h3>Can I run a safeguarding audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The manager or safeguarding lead receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo of the record, and the report and action list generate automatically. No separate app or login is required, and no case detail about a named resident is entered, because quickler is not a clinical or safeguarding record.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-compliance-checklist-monthly-uk.html\">Monthly care home compliance checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-plan-audit-review-uk.html\">Care plan audit and review</a></li></ul>"}
